+## C. Concrete zero-local-tool, one-relay configurations
+
+### C1. Common boundary and vocabulary
+
+The following are **proposed generated policies**, not implemented launcher changes. Let **R** be a pinned, finite relay tool manifest. For a four-tool lab manifest `{read_file,write_file,apply_patch,bash}`, Pi names become `mcp__swarma_remote_tools__<name>`; OpenCode names become `swarma-remote-tools_<name>`. Naming comes from the pinned clients, not from guessing a common MCP convention. The relay declaration uses the neutral absolute path **`/opt/curator/qualified/relay/swarma-remote-tools`**; this is an example managed path, not a path on the research host. [P-mcp-names] [P-mcp-tools] [O-mcp-names] [R-claude]
+
+Before launch, a trusted supervisor must verify executable/dependency digests, protected policy files, exactly one server, exact R and schemas, agent/provider/model selection, allowed environment, and OS policy. Unknown/malformed/unreadable sources, failed MCP readiness, an unexpected connected server, changed tool list, or stale policy generation must refuse. MCP clients' own error-and-continue behavior is not that supervisor: Pi skips invalid entries; OpenCode can report failed connections. Never interpret these as a successfully empty configuration. [C8] [C9] [P-mcp-config] [P-mcp] [O-mcp]
+
+Only the protected policy tree is immutable; permitted session/cache/log state has separate writable roots. The harness cannot replace policy through a writable parent, symlink, config RPC, extension, updater, or local tool. The entire process and descendants remain inside CIP-0009 confinement; provider networking is limited by the trusted proxy/egress policy, and the relay's only effective route is the bridge. This is a proposed composition of the existing CIP requirements, not a new guarantee furnished by either tool flag. [C8] [C9]
