+## D. Workarounds and the limits of their guarantees
+
+The owner rule has two tests: **remove every locally acting model tool**, then **bound the local activity of the remaining process**. The following judgments apply to that rule; they are not claims that a sandbox makes arbitrary third-party code trustworthy. CIP-0008 R8 explicitly treats the kernel boundary separately from launch flags and does not attest a malicious donor's kernel. [C8] [C9]
+
+| Technique | What it can guarantee, with stated prerequisites | Verdict under the owner rule |
+|---|---|---|
+| Pi native positive allowlist | Removes unlisted tool definitions and executors in both examined releases; 1.1.0 can retain only its native MCP extension's relay tools. [P-session] [P0-session] [P-loop] | A source-supported removal mechanism. Candidate with the sealed sources and runtime closure in §C2; measurement still required. |
+| Pi SDK with our registry | Exact `tools` names, only our `customTools`, and no-discovery loader can create a closed registry. [P-sdk-doc] [P0-sdk-doc] [P-full-control] | A valid architectural workaround; the 0.84.2 one-MCP adapter must first be implemented and audited. It is not an already guaranteed off-the-shelf solution. |
+| Pi operation substitution | `ReadOperations` and corresponding tool factories permit remote implementations. The shipped SSH example wraps read/write/edit/bash, starts a local SSH process, and **falls back to local executors** when remote mode is absent. [P-read-ops] [P-ssh-example] | The example is **not** the required workaround: wrong relay transport and local fallback. A purpose-built adapter could qualify only if all operations use the one MCP relay and absence/error refuses; prefer removing the local tools entirely. |
+| OpenCode deny-all + exact relay allows | Whole-tool policy is applied when building each provider-request tool/executor map. [O-permission] [O-request] [O-llm] | Candidate if agent/session/config inputs are immutable for that run. A granular deny, permission prompt or plugin hook alone does not meet the offered-tool requirement. |
+| OpenCode SDK/own registry | The public SDK calls the server; the internal V2 registry has materialization controls. [O-doc-sdk] [O-v2-registry] | A public SDK client is not itself a workaround. An audited V2 host with explicit services could qualify, but this research does not claim an existing closed embedding or reuse the native row for it. |
+| Whole-process OS sandbox | Bounds reads, writes, subprocess authority, network and IPC only to the extent covered by the selected, tested profile and OS. Existing CIP requirements and the pinned sandbox implementation illustrate the required surfaces. [C9] [R-sandbox] | Necessary defense for either candidate's non-tool local activity. **Insufficient on its own** for a harness that still offers unavoidable locally acting tools. |
+| Wrapper/supervisor | Can own argv, environment, protected roots, policy generation and transport; reject unexpected tool/server manifests before a model request. This is the composition required by CIP-0008/0009. [C8] [C9] | A guarantee only with a closed input grammar, independent policy identity and the real execution path inside the OS boundary. PATH stubs, prompt instructions, regex output filters and an after-dispatch tripwire do not remove a callable executor. |
+
+**macOS:** propose a separate, versioned Seatbelt profile applied to the **harness and every descendant**, with explicit runtime reads, managed writable state, one relay/bridge channel, admitted provider traffic, and denied donor data/IPC. The existing `remote-worker-harness/internal/tools/sandbox.go` is a **bash wrapper**, not a ready-made whole-harness qualification. It starts from `allow default`, applies read/write/network/Mach-service denials, and refuses when the Seatbelt launcher is absent. Its source is evidence of a design and refusal path, not proof that a new Pi/OpenCode process has no escape. Reusing it unchanged would leave a scope gap. Qualify the full profile and actual OS build, or refuse that platform row. [R-sandbox] [C9]
+
+**Linux:** Landlock 6.12 documentation says restrictions apply to a thread and its descendants; apply the boundary before launching the multithreaded runtime. Require the needed ABI/access rights instead of silently weakening the profile on an older kernel. Its network controls cover TCP ports, not a destination-host egress policy; use an additional network boundary and close inherited descriptors. These are design requirements to test, not an assertion about the runner's kernel support. [Landlock-6.12]
+
+Bubblewrap **v0.11.0**, commit `9ca3b05ec787acfb4b17bed37db5719fa777834f`, provides a namespace/mount sandbox building block; upstream says the caller's configuration defines the security policy. Proposed configuration: minimal read-only runtime mounts, protected policy mount, private writable state, separate PID/network namespaces as needed, and explicitly passed bridge/provider channels. A broad donor-home mount, inherited host socket, or full-root read mount would defeat the intended read boundary. Pair it with the measured syscall/egress policy; “runs in bubblewrap” is not a qualification result. [Bubblewrap-0.11.0] [C9]
+
+For both platforms, a tool named `read_file` or `bash` can be acceptable **only when its admitted implementation forwards to the remote workplace**. Names alone do not prove locality. The plan must bind the relay implementation, tool manifest, bridge channel and server count; otherwise a local MCP server exporting reassuring names is still a local capability. [C8] [C9]
