+| File contents and directory reads | Trusted syscall/file-access observation plus synthetic canary bytes. Linux may use a privileged `strace -ff` collector outside the harness identity; macOS needs an available privileged file/process collector whose coverage is proven by a separate control process. Hash protected files before/after as a separate write check. | No successful out-of-bound read or enumeration, no protected-file mutation; all managed writes inventoried. Unchanged hashes **do not prove no reads**. If hosted macOS cannot supply adequate observation because of platform restrictions, mark that cell unqualified; do not replace it with an empty log. |
+| Processes and executable loads | Observe harness/relay descendant trees and exec attempts. A synthetic helper produces a known marker in an observer control run; the candidate run must show only the admitted process closure. | No unapproved child, shell helper, plugin/package installer, updater or language server. Count both denied attempts and successes, with the expected relay startup separately attributed. |
+| Network | Trusted packet/socket/process observation plus fake-provider and relay logs; include DNS, IPv4/IPv6, TCP/UDP and local-socket paths. Exercise an explicit forbidden endpoint using a synthetic helper to prove the boundary. | Only the admitted provider/bridge channels; no telemetry/catalog/download/share traffic. A proxy log alone cannot prove absence of bypass traffic. Do not equate “no successful connection” with “no attempt.” |
+| macOS IPC / platform services | Use synthetic control helpers to test denied service classes named in the sandbox policy, including clipboard/application control; do not read real user data. | Known policy and collector coverage for each admitted platform class. Unobservable or untested classes remain unknown and block promotion of a row claiming them. [C9] [R-sandbox] |
+| State/log integrity | Hash immutable policy before/after; measure writable bytes/paths and verify quota/retention policy. Observer artifacts live outside harness-writable roots. | Harness logs cannot attest their own safety or update the trusted policy/measurement record. Account for allowed diagnostic/session/cache writes instead of reporting “zero file effects.” [C8] [C9] |
+
+The collector itself must pass a startup self-check: it must observe one known allowed action and one known denied action for every category it claims to cover. Apply the sandbox to a **synthetic capability probe** as well as the harness, because removed tools may never reach the kernel denial. Close inherited descriptors and test symlink/path traversal. Whole-process confinement is independently necessary even when all forced model calls are rejected. The platform mechanisms and their limitations are described in §D; this table is the proposed measurement method, not a claim that any collector has run. [C9] [Landlock-6.12] [Bubblewrap-0.11.0]
+
+### F5. Negatives that make a green result meaningful
+
+Use named controlled mutants; run each in an isolated synthetic environment and require the corresponding gate to fail. **Narrow the policy**, rather than only deleting it:
+
+| Mutant / failed input | Gate that must reject |
+|---|---|
+| Add Pi `read` to the explicit relay allowlist while leaving shell removed | Offered-set equality detects the extra schema; forced-read control demonstrates that the observation would catch a readable canary. |
+| Change OpenCode catch-all deny into only `bash:"deny"`, retaining relay allows | Offered-set equality detects remaining local tools. A shell-only test must not be mistaken for full removal. |
+| Add a second MCP server, collide after name sanitization, or change one allowed schema/executor identity | Server/manifest/source identity check fails before any model request or unauthorized server start. |
+| Re-enable one project source/custom-tool directory or replace a protected policy parent with a writable link | Effective-source/immutability gate fails; an import/read marker exposes an accidentally bypassed gate. |
+| Supply a permissive agent/session permission, raw RPC command, alternate run transport or caller-tail tool flag | The real composer/intake grammar refuses; no native binary launch with the widened policy. |
+| Narrow the sandbox to deny writes but permit canary reads | Independent read-boundary probe fails even when file hashes are unchanged. |
+| Change policy generation, executable digest, catalog, profile or relay identity after composition | At-exec identity/generation check refuses; a later resume tuple must test the same at-resume path. |
+| Drop/truncate capture, make a config read fail, return malformed tool-list JSON, or forge a harness-owned “passed” record | Audit/source evidence is unknown or invalid and the qualification gate refuses; no absence fallback. |
+| Disable an observer hook or place its output in harness-writable state | Observer control/action counters or integrity gate fails; no self-attestation. |
+
+These are proposed tests of the boundaries identified in the cited production paths and CIP requirements, not implemented tests. Record a coverage fraction for every family: offered names checked / enumerated source names, forced calls observed / scheduled cases, source layers exercised / inventoried layers, side-effect controls passed / required controls, and mutants killed / introduced. Report provider/feature branches, kernel classes or paths outside coverage explicitly. Current runtime coverage from this research is **0/4 native candidate cells qualified; 0 runtime experiments executed**. [P-session] [O-request] [O-config] [C8] [C9]
+
+### F6. Evidence packaging and promotion rule
+
+Run each future gate as a standalone process, with bounded groups comfortably below the headless command limit. Capture its **real exit code** without a status-masking pipe. A deliberately failing mutant remains a nonzero gate result with its expected-failure rationale; a separate assertion may pass when that failure is observed. Never mark the failing command itself green.
+
+For each candidate cell, attach: exact tuple and generated bytes; binary/archive/dependency hashes; source/runtime version inventory; effective-source inventory; relay handshake and server count; every decoded provider tool set; forced-call outcomes; process/file/network/IPC observations and declared blind spots; positive/negative controls and coverage ratios; per-command exit codes; refusal classifications; and bounded state footprint. Sanitize generated artifacts before public attachment. Attach evidence before ending the run.
+
+**Promotion rule:** reviewers may add a CIP-0008 row only when the production entry path and its negative controls pass for that exact tuple, all required observation reads are valid, and no extra locally acting tool or side effect escapes the declared boundary. A different release, model adapter/catalog, transport, persistence mode, policy or OS profile requires requalification. This follows the existing CIP acceptance model; this report changes neither the CIP nor launcher support. [C8]
